<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Separable Differential Equations</title>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Over time, a sample of a radioactive material will decay at a
  rate proportional to its mass.  If we call <m>M(t)</m> the mass of the
  radioactive substance, we can express this fact by
  <md>
  \frac{dM}{dt}=-kM.
  </md></p>
        <p>Find the general solution of the differential equation.  (Your
  solutions will depend on the constant of proportionality <m>k</m>.)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now find the solution where the initial mass <m>M(0)=M_0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The half-life of a radioactive substance is the amount of time
  required for half the mass to decay.  Carbon-14 has a half-life of
  5730 years.  What is the value of <m>k</m> for Carbon-14?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If the mass of a Carbon-14 sample is initially 20 grams, how long
  does it take until there are only 5 grams left?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose you have an investment account that earns interest at
  the rate of  7% compounded continuously.  Call <m>M(t)</m> the amount of
  money in the account.  This means that <m>M</m> changes at the rate of
  7% of <m>M</m>.  Write this fact as a differential equation that
  describes how the amount of money changes</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now suppose that you withdraw money at the rate of $10,000 dollars a
year from the account.  How does this change your differential
equation?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the general solution to this differential equation.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the solution that corresponds to an initial value of $100,000 in
the account.  How long does it take  
until all the money has been withdrawn?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the smallest amount of money you would need to live off the
interest indefinitely?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>When a drug is present in
  a patient's blood stream, it is typically absorbed by the body (and
  hence taken out of the bloodstream) at a
  rate proportional to the amount in the blood stream.</p>
        <p>Let <m>Q(t)</m> be the amount of a drug in a patient's bloodstream.
  Write a differential equation that expresses the fact that the rate
  at which the drug leaves the bloodstream is proportional to the
  amount in the bloodstream.  Use <m>k</m> as a constant of
  proportionality.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the solution to the differential equation with an initial
  amount <m>Q(0) = Q_0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If the half-life of a particular drug is 10 hours, find the constant
  <m>k</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now suppose that a drug is being given intraveneously at the rate of
  2 milligrams per hour.  Write a differential equation that describes
  the rate of change of <m>Q</m>.  (Just we have seen before, the rate of
  change is the rate at which the drug enters the bloodstream minus
  the rate at which it leaves.)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Assuming that the drug is not initially present in the patient,
  solve the differential equation for <m>Q</m> and explain what happens
  after a long time.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
